===Etymology 1===
{{etyl|gem-pro|ang}} {{recons|armaz|lang=gem-pro}}, from {{etyl|ine-pro|ang}} {{recons|armo-|lang=ine-pro}}, from ''*ar-, *arə-'' ‘fit, join’.  Cognate with Old Frisian {{term|arm}}, Old Saxon {{term|arm}} (Dutch {{term|arm|lang=nl}}), Old High German {{term|arm}} (German {{term|Arm}}), Old Norse {{term|armr}} (Swedish {{term|arm|lang=sv}}), Gothic {{term|𐌰𐍂𐌼𐍃}}.  The [[Proto-Indo-European]] root is also the source of Old Church Slavonic {{term|рамо}} (Russian {{term|рамо||shoulder}}), Latin {{term|armus}}.

